#{[17592186163300 "Let's once again consider the line integral with respect to arc length of $f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nThere are two more important properties of this type of line integral that we need to be aware of.\n\n- There is usually more than one way to parametrize a curve. However, line integrals with respect to arc length are *independent of the choice of parametrization*. In other words, we get the same result regardless of which parametrization we choose.\n\nFor example, the paths $C_1$ and $C_2,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}t⟩, & 0\\le t<2\\pi  \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}2u,\\,\\operatorname{sin}2u⟩, & 0\\le u<\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nboth traverse a circle of radius $1$ once in the counterclockwise direction starting from $(1,0).$ However, since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of parametrization, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_2} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\n- Line integrals with respect to arc length are also *independent of the orientation* of $C.$ For example, suppose that $C$ is a path along a particular curve traversed from the point $A$ to the point $B.$ Then, $-C$ is a path along the same curve but traversed from $B$ to $A,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4f68e10429fa4bda69366751c303216d27df995edf2502b61ab9bfe84c3ff45.png)\n\nIndependence of orientation means that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{-C} f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\n- Finally, it's important to mention that all of the properties discussed in this lesson apply to line integrals with respect to arc length over space curves, i.e., line integrals of the form $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nTo demonstrate point 2 above, consider the line integral\n\\[\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s, \n\\]\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $A(0,0)$ to $B(2,2).$ \n\nOne possible parameterization of $C$ from $A$ to $B$ is $\\mathbf r = \\langle t, t\\rangle$ for $t\\in[0,2].$ We get $x=y=t$ on the curve, and $\\mathbf r ' = \\langle 1, 1\\rangle$ which leads to $||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.$ Therefore, compuing the line integral gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 t\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIf we *reverse* the direction of the curve, traversing from $B$ to $A,$ we get the parametrization \n\\[\n\\mathbf r(0+2-t) = \\mathbf r(2-t) =   \\langle 2-t,2- t\\rangle,\\quad t \\in[0,2].\n\\]\nNow, we get $x=y=2-t, \\mathbf r' = \\langle -1, -1\\rangle$ and so $||\\mathbf r'|| = \\sqrt 2.$ Computing the line integral using this parametrization gives\n\\[\n\\int\\limits_C x\\,\\textrm d s = \\sqrt 2\\int_0^2 (2-t)\\,\\textrm{d}t = 2\\sqrt 2.\n\\]"]}
